{"ahead_by":15,"behind_by":0,"commits":[{"date":"2026-11-15T02:11:49Z","message":"demo: a raw factory-edge demo in one short script (#5523)\n\nThree workloads run side by side, each allowed one destination. The maintenance\nworkload asks for one it was not given; the host refuses and records it, mvmctl\nexplain shows it, and the two inspection workloads carry on. This is beats 1 to 4\nand 6 of the proposed factory-edge presentation, with no assertions, staging or\nteardown machinery: about 25 lines of logic that print the demo.\n\nThe workloads are started one after another, because starting them together can\nfail with 'another local volume lifecycle operation is already in progress'.\n\nThe guide is half a page and maps each step to the mvm-client call the Studio UI\nwould use. That table is from reading the library, not from running it, and it\nrecords one gap found on the way: the library cannot report what a machine is\nallowed to reach (#5521).","sha":"03df52b2572cb39562b0e508258023e3cf820594"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:25:18Z","message":"fix(client): launches without volumes no longer serialize on the volume lock (#5525)\n\nEvery persistent launch took the host-wide volume lifecycle lock with a\nnon-blocking acquire, so starting several machines at once failed with\n'another local volume lifecycle operation is already in progress' even when\nnone of them used a volume. A launch with no explicit volumes and no\nregistered mounts touches no shared state and now skips the lock.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","sha":"f5edef7879c33971e0706fd3218a59ae9aa1ab75"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:41:05Z","message":"Add explicit cold and warm density harness lanes (#5517)\n\n* feat: distinguish cold and warm density launches\n\n* fix(density): support empty option arrays on macOS bash\n\n* fix(density): prime isolated homes before waves\n\n* fix(density): retain resident wave measurements without receipts","sha":"804f33dde9b2bc77af67325442f073e45188f0c9"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:41:07Z","message":"docs: reserve schema 9 for production composition (#5516)","sha":"b45ab8396709938146fa12973f3787d97da88251"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:41:08Z","message":"docs(contracts): mark the Firecracker lifecycle owner contract as deferred and link its tracking issue (#4839) (#5524)","sha":"f1e3cccb70653313e5913bba4c7fcde69f7ded85"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:41:10Z","message":"fix: deliver canonical runtime environment to boot commands (#5518)\n\nCompose the existing host-owned workload proxy, CA and placeholder environment into launch commands. Refuse differing caller overrides without logging values and validate machine names before readiness or state access. Preserve DevOnly admission, the environment denylist and sealed-guest refusal. Cover canonical delivery, collisions, caller configuration and early naming errors. No new owner, API, policy gate or Studio pin change. Related to #4839.","sha":"75c9e44234a165eb75c557b4d63bc8a094b2388c"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:41:16Z","message":"docs: record VM-scoped mailbox key custody (#5513)","sha":"f80b7c89ced6e239ba2e37479a29d295b94c5158"},{"date":"2026-11-15T02:41:19Z","message":"perf(trace): span the runtime-proof verification, the lifecycle bind and its flushes (#5506)\n\nA signed-release launch reaches verify_release_archive from the overlay\nattach, the initramfs attach, admission and the lifecycle bind, and the\nbind's snapshot copies and flushes run before the launch trace starts, so\nneither shows up in a phase table or a span report today.\n\nDeclare spans on the verification and each of its steps (read and digest,\nsignature checks, temporary snapshot, member check), on the lifecycle\nbind, on each per-file snapshot copy and on its durability barrier, and\non sync_file and sync_dir. The read, the temporary snapshot and the\nbarrier move into small functions so each has its own span; the bytes\nread, the checks made and the order they are made in are unchanged.\n\nWith MVM_SPAN_TIMINGS=1 the report now gives calls and time for each, so\none launch on a Mac shows how many times the archive is verified and how\nmuch of vmm_create is flush time.\n\nRefs #5487, #4391, #4648.","sha":"401ad4c9bab3b954b52c39a84c4d010f4e998650"},{"date":"2026-11-15T03:03:39Z","message":"fix(agentd): keep the entrypoint descriptor across exec for a script warm worker (#5503)\n\n* test(agentd): a script warm worker started through the descriptor path must run\n\n* fix(agentd): keep the entrypoint descriptor across exec for a script warm worker\n\nA warm worker started through /proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e has to keep that descriptor\nacross exec when it is an interpreter script: the kernel re-execs the\ninterpreter on that path, so a close-on-exec descriptor is gone by the time\nthe interpreter reopens the script. The worker spawn kept the descriptor\nthrough the hygiene pass but left it close-on-exec, so a script worker died\nwith \"/bin/sh: cannot open /proc/self/fd/3\". The function-entrypoint\nwrappers (#!/usr/bin/env python3 and node) take this path.\n\nClear close-on-exec on that one descriptor in the worker's child before exec,\nas the cold path does with its non-close-on-exec copy. Only the descriptor\nthe worker is run from is kept; every other inherited descriptor is still\nclosed. The previous commit's test failed on Linux CI with exactly this error.\n\n* fix(agentd): keep the entrypoint descriptor only for a script worker, and witness it\n\nOnly an interpreter script needs the entrypoint descriptor to survive exec:\nthe kernel re-execs its interpreter on /proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e. A binary is read\nin the first exec. Peek at the first two bytes with pread, which leaves the\nshared file offset alone, and clear close-on-exec only for a #! file. A file\nthat cannot be read counts as not a script, so its descriptor stays\nclose-on-exec.\n\nTests: a binary worker does not inherit a descriptor to its own binary, the\npeek recognises only a #! file and does not move the offset, and a privileged\nwitness shows a script worker started through the descriptor path still holds\nno capabilities and has no_new_privs. The witness joins the privileged lane.\nThe existing capability witness and the new ones share their setup helpers.\n\n* test(agentd): give the binary-worker probe a control so it cannot pass by erroring","sha":"8a7f4d85bd98a9bd048ce11bc6d91fd092485c2e"},{"date":"2026-11-15T03:42:14Z","message":"demo: start the three workloads at once and show the audit record (#5530)\n\n* demo: start the three workloads at once and show the audit record\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e\n\n* docs(trail): record this week's session learnings\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","sha":"059b915f25c5db0550d9210b2fac7269e21f6cbe"},{"date":"2026-11-15T03:47:59Z","message":"docs: remove the factory-edge staging harness in favour of the simple demo (#5529)\n\nThe staging, live-demo and fake-mvmctl test scripts are replaced by\nscripts/factory-edge-demo.sh. The contract's runbook section now points at it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","sha":"f7a9759af1161c4f7182f8af9ac1b951fd3f7eab"},{"date":"2026-11-15T04:03:58Z","message":"fix(ci): restore the BDD and aarch64 CI witnesses on main (#5511)\n\nCI (main) has failed twice in a row on 50b1a239b7 with three jobs:\nBDD conformance (2 scenarios), Test workspace (aarch64), and the\nmeta-lane that requires every lane to pass.\n\n- The operations cookbook's TypeScript example still assigned a bare\n  string env value; #5463 made EnvValue explicit, so the documented\n  example no longer typechecks. Write MODE: literal(\"dev\") and import\n  literal.\n- mvmctl policy validate ./policy/api-group.toml is newly documented\n  and sat outside the coverage ledger. Add a scenario that stages the\n  guide's own group file in a scratch directory and validates it\n  offline, then regenerate the ledger: the policy-validate examples\n  move from uncovered debt to covered, and entries whose doc lines\n  recent PRs removed leave the ledger.\n- a_console_spawned_shell_holds_no_capabilities read the pty master\n  with read_to_end, which propagates EIO; EIO is how a pty master\n  reports that every slave holder has closed it, so a fast-exiting\n  shell failed the read on the aarch64 runner. Treat EIO as end of\n  stream, matching pump_output's contract.\n\nRefs #5369","sha":"24f4c6641bca5d3b976d4d44649fef6afcf513ad"},{"date":"2026-11-15T04:17:39Z","message":"refactor(hostd): regroup the supervisor modules by responsibility (#4839) (#5531)\n\nMove the 65 flat modules under crates/mvm-hostd/src/supervisor/ into\nadmission, audit, datapath, endpoint, inspection, lifecycle and telemetry\ngroups. Pure move: no item bodies, signatures or tests change; module\npaths, path strings in gates and docs follow the new layout.\n\nRefs #4839","sha":"b6368b3d3f767f8f5ca8782227db3ae8d8604618"},{"date":"2026-11-15T04:19:45Z","message":"feat(install): support uninstall through install.sh (#5505)","sha":"faffdad38b032cf2344aaef440784425b47a1cdf"},{"date":"2026-11-15T04:20:07Z","message":"feat(hvf): release memory a restored guest wrote and then freed (#5509)\n\nA restored guest's RAM is a private file mapping, and the balloon device\nskipped every span in one, so a warm-claimed guest never returned memory.\nThe device now asks the backend whether a file-backed span holds private\ncopies and, when it does, releases it like anonymous memory. Unwritten\nfile pages are still left alone.","sha":"5a27c39c2e4e265d0741daf46ea9b4949dbb0820"}],"files":[{"additions":1,"deletions":0,"filename":".github/workflows/ci-main.yml","status":"modified"},{"additions":2,"deletions":2,"filename":".github/workflows/ci.yml","status":"modified"},{"additions":2,"deletions":3,"filename":".github/workflows/installer-compat.yml","st